Verdict: The pipeline is correct — no blind_inst instruction is added under --condition blind. The model receives a blank image 
  and a question ending in \nAnswer: with no guidance on what to do.                                                              
                                                                                                                                  
  ---                                                                                                                             
  One real issue                                                                                                                  
                                                                                                                                  
  The prompt still contains "Answer the question using a single word or phrase." — this is baked into the question format in      
  VQADataset_json, not controlled by condition. This may suppress abstentions because the model is explicitly told to answer. If  
  you want to measure natural abstention rates, you might want a separate condition that strips this instruction, e.g. just       
  "Question: {q}\nAnswer:" for blind.                                                                                             
                                                                                                                                  
  ---                                                                                                                             
  Abstain analysis outline                                                                                                        
                                                                                                                                  
  What to measure: Does the model recognize it can't answer from a blank image, and what predicts whether it tries anyway?        
                                                                                                                                  
  Step 1 — Abstention detection             
  Classify each blind output into:                                                                                                
  - abstained — refusal phrase: "I cannot", "I don't see", "no image", "unable to determine", "I'm not sure", "cannot answer"     
  - hallucinated_correct — output matches GT despite blank image                                                                  
  - hallucinated_wrong — plausible VQA-style answer, doesn't match GT                                                             
  - degenerate — empty, \n, or "Answer:" repeated                                                                                 

  refusal_re = re.compile(r"cannot|can't|don't see|no image|unable|not sure|unclear|n/a", re.I)

  Step 2 — Abstention rate by tier

  Cross-tabulate abstained rate against A–E tiers. Hypothesis: Tier A questions (model has strong priors) will have the lowest
  abstention rate — the model hallucinates confidently even with a blank image. Tier D/E questions may have higher abstention
  because the referential anchor (the entity name) is what makes the model confident.

  Step 3 — Abstention rate by op

  Text-reading questions (op=text) should abstain most — a model legitimately can't read a sign from a blank image. Count and
  attribute questions will hallucinate more freely. This is your cleanest operationalization of "which cognitive operations are
  grounded vs. prior-driven."

  Step 4 — Confidence on abstained vs. answered outputs

  Compare mean(confidence) between abstained and answered outputs. Hypothesis: answered outputs have higher mean token probability
   — the model commits more strongly when it confabulates. This lets you tie logprob calibration back to abstention behavior.
Step 5 — Compare blind vs inst condition directly

  For the same question, what fraction of models:
  - Answered blind → answered inst (stable hallucination — pure text prior)
  - Abstained blind → answered inst (instruction unlocks the prior)
  - Abstained blind → abstained inst (genuinely hard question even with permission)

  The blind→inst flip group is the most interesting: it means the model knows the answer when allowed to use priors but suppresses
   it without explicit permission. This is a strong signal that the inst answers come from training statistics, not visual
  grounding.

  Step 6 — Tie to tier

  A gold question in Tier A that: (a) gives a confident wrong answer in standard evaluation, (b) abstains under blind, (c)
  confidently answers under inst — is the canonical example of a "corpus prior" question for the human study.
